kinetic-energy-density-term-eab15cc7.conceptual_definition:
The kinetic energy density term, defined as $- rac{\hbar^2}{2m} |
abla \psi|^2 = - rac{\hbar^2}{2m} 
abla \psi^* \cdot 
abla \psi$, accounts for the energy stored in spatial gradients of the quantum matter field $\psi(\mathbf{r}, t)$. In the Euler-Lagrange field equations, integrating this spatial derivative term by parts produces the Laplacian term $- rac{\hbar^2}{2m}
abla^2 \psi$, yielding the canonical quantum kinetic energy operator $\hat{T} = - rac{\hbar^2}{2m}
abla^2$ in the Schrödinger equation.

